{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of R14 retirement 1 of 2, the ScopeService entrypoint, at exact head 06a75de328fef1f28214b76bf5ebf71b52fec652 (tree 0e1e11d142b00016699489eaa447d698a8046c3a), for request db60a6f9, under builder promise bf31905c. Verdict: APPROVED.\n\nI read the implementation note 40b89f59 first, then the request. Then I read every change from Main d6967b7f: worker.ts, the five test files, the entry-point inventory and its test, and the three documents.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- Base and delta. The head descends from Main d6967b7f through 3 commits. They change exactly the 13 paths named by the 13 artifacts, one artifact per path. None of the artifacts has been superseded. The worktree was clean at the head.\n- Gate. The gate at c0fd56eb (tree 63369136) exited 0, with the source unchanged before and after. The output ends \"gate passed\". Results:\n  - plain: 2892 passed and 1 skipped;\n  - Native: 822 passed and 2 skipped.\n\n  From c0fd56eb to the head, only the note and its manifest change, so no new gate is owed.\n- Focused runs. The focused Native run passed 28 of 28 cases in 7 files.\n- Evidence:\n  - The committed manifest has SHA-256 c2c455a9…. All 88 entries match their recorded size and hash, and no entry is writable.\n  - SHA256SUMS has 87 lines, all OK.\n  - The report tail's SHA256SUMS has SHA-256 8acd2da1…, as stated. Its 30 lines are all OK, it holds 31 files, and none is writable.\n- The request's witnesses:\n  - `git grep ScopeService` at the head finds nothing outside notes/.\n  - The gate passed.\n  - The inventory test requires the production WorkerEntrypoint exports to be exactly `[\"ApplicationScreenService\"]`.\n- Documents:\n  - check-doc-links on an export of the head: 723 links, 0 broken.\n  - check-notes d6967b7f..06a75de3: 0 warnings.\n  - check-commit-messages: 3 checked, 0 flagged.\n  - `git diff --check`: clean.\n- Configuration. No Wrangler configuration changes. No configuration in the repository binds a `ScopeService` entrypoint. The sandbox binds `ApplicationScreenService`.\n\nI reran no suite.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe production change only removes code:\n- the `ScopeService` class (14 forwarding methods over `api(indexedScopes(env.SCOPES, env), null, env)`);\n- its doc comment;\n- the mentions in worker.ts, docs/scopes.md, docs/security/entry-points.md and packages/scope/README.md.\n\nNothing in production called it. The page, the MCP routes and `route` already use `api()` in process. docs/architecture.md did not name the entrypoint, so it is unchanged, as the request allows.\n\nThe test Worker's `TestApiEntry` is the removed class moved into test code. It has the same 14 methods, the same default `scopes()` (indexed lookup), the same null address and the same `env`. `NetService` and `PlatformService` still override `scopes()` with the raw NET and PLATFORM fixture namespaces, as before. `ServingTestService` keeps the indexed default with its own index.\n\nDropping the default arguments in the moved methods changes no behavior. `api().create` and `api().submit` declare the same defaults (`definitions = []`, `beside = {}`).\n\nconformance.types.ts now checks `NetService`, which extends `TestApiEntry`, against `ScopeApi` in both directions, and that the binding is a `ServiceBinding`. The test compiler passed. Production imports no test code.\n\nThe seal table changes only the `$module` hash and drops the `ScopeService` row. The other 27 declaration seals are the same as at H1.\n\n## Security\n\n- The change removes an RPC surface and adds none. The only production WorkerEntrypoint export is now `ApplicationScreenService`, with its sealed method list.\n- Addressless admission is unchanged. In-process API calls and hosted fetch still supply a null address and ignore forwarded headers, and the documents now say so without the retired name.\n- The two tests that measured the addressless join and serving windows through the old class now measure them through the test bindings. These call the same production `api()` and the same production serving objects, so the witnesses still test production admission.\n- New check. `workerEntrypoints` parses worker.ts and refuses export forms it cannot classify:\n  - `export *`;\n  - re-exports and specifier lists;\n  - a default export that is not an object;\n  - exported declarations other than classes, functions, interfaces and types;\n  - ambiguous classes and non-identifier bases.\n\n  It follows local base chains to the one imported `WorkerEntrypoint`. The neutral control appends `export class UnexpectedEntry extends WorkerEntrypoint {}` to the source text. `servedInventory` then fails with \"Production WorkerEntrypoint exports\".\n\nOne point, not blocking. A class whose base is imported is counted as not an entrypoint, for example `DeployedScope extends ScopeObject`. So an export extending a `WorkerEntrypoint` subclass imported from another module would not be counted by this check. The `$module` seal still catches any change to worker.ts's imports or exports, so such a change cannot pass review unseen. If the check is meant to stand alone, it could refuse imported bases outside a short allowlist.\n\nDeployment. The planner's next deploy removes the export from the three room Workers. No configuration binds it, so nothing breaks.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThis is a net removal: about 30 production lines and two doc passages. The new inventory function is 44 lines, a proportionate cost for a check that refuses a second RPC entrypoint. The test facade repeats 14 one-line forwarders. Keeping them explicit lets the conformance type check stay meaningful. I see nothing to cut.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved. One RPC entrypoint has been removed. Admission and the null address are unchanged, and the witnesses still test production `api()`. The imported-base gap is noted, and the module seal covers it.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- Request conditions:\n  - one head, with source and documents;\n  - ordinary gate passed;\n  - the grep, gate and inventory witnesses hold;\n  - no new setting or redeploy step;\n  - public wording names no other product;\n  - order: after G1 (248edb41) and H1 (d6967b7f), before 7ee9a63b.\n\nThe landing check will follow the landing. R14 2/2 (fe2305a2, with planner amendment 999ffe93) follows separately.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:40b89f598d589c122bb0f1d30ae3c53f7afa3ea7","binding":"assigned","head":"06a75de328fef1f28214b76bf5ebf71b52fec652","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:059835d8077d9693a2d5446d37566cfd88966143\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:bf31905c358163deb23f131f96bc5044611b2438\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:059835d8077d9693a2d5446d37566cfd88966143","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}